<body>
<div class="app">
  <div class="backstrip"><a href="spanish-gcse-ccea-grammar-spine.html">← Back to the grammar spine</a></div>
  <div class="topbar">
    <div class="logo"><span class="spark">🗣️</span> The Present Tense</div>
    <div class="bar"><i id="bar"></i></div>
    <div class="score">⭐ <span id="score">0</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="spec">CCEA GCSE Spanish · Grammar spine 2 of 10 — The present-tense system</div>
  <div class="stage" id="stage">

    <!-- 0 WELCOME -->
    <section class="screen active">
      <span class="tag">Mini-Lesson</span>
      <h1>The present-tense system</h1>
      <p class="big">The present tense is the engine of Spanish — you will use it in every theme, every paper. This lesson builds it from the ground up: <b>regular verbs</b>, the <b>stem-changers</b>, the <b>core irregulars</b>, the crucial <b>ser vs estar</b> split, and the <b>present continuous</b>.</p>
      <figure>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 560 130" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="One Spanish present-tense form covers three English meanings">
          <rect x="18" y="45" width="150" height="52" rx="14" fill="#d9ebff" stroke="#2c2840" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="93" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#0b5bbf">hablo</text>
          <text x="93" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Nunito" font-size="12" fill="#0b5bbf">one Spanish form</text>
          <line x1="168" y1="71" x2="360" y2="35" stroke="#12a67a" stroke-width="3"/>
          <line x1="168" y1="71" x2="360" y2="71" stroke="#12a67a" stroke-width="3"/>
          <line x1="168" y1="71" x2="360" y2="107" stroke="#12a67a" stroke-width="3"/>
          <text x="366" y="40" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">I speak</text>
          <text x="366" y="76" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">I am speaking</text>
          <text x="366" y="112" font-family="Fredoka" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#2c2840">I do speak</text>
        </svg>
      </figure>
      <p>Work through each screen and answer the <b>questions</b> as you go — some ask you to <b>recognise</b> the right form, some ask you to <b>produce</b> it by typing. Collect ⭐ stars and press <b>Start</b>.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 1 WHAT IT DOES -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">What the present tense does</span>
      <h2>One tense, three English ideas</h2>
      <p>A single Spanish present-tense verb can be translated three ways in English. There is <b>no separate word</b> for "am/do":</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl v">Estudio</span> español. → I <b>study</b> Spanish.</li>
        <li><span class="hl v">Estudio</span> español. → I <b>am studying</b> Spanish.</li>
        <li>Sí, <span class="hl v">estudio</span> español. → I <b>do study</b> Spanish.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note"><b>Why this matters:</b> beginners often try to translate "am" with a word (<span class="es">soy</span>) and write <span class="hl r"><s>soy estudio</s></span>. Don't — the ending <span class="es">-o</span> already carries "I". The verb does the whole job.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2 Q MCQ meaning -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Quick check</span>
      <h2>Reading the present</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">?</span><span>A text says: <b><span class="es">María trabaja en un hospital.</span></b> Which English translation is <b>possible</b>?</span></div>
        <div class="opts" data-q="q1" data-why="Spanish has one present tense, so 'trabaja' can be 'works', 'is working' or 'does work'. All three are valid depending on context.">
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Only "María worked in a hospital" <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">Only "María will work in a hospital" <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="1">Either "works" or "is working" in a hospital <span class="mk">✅</span></button>
          <button class="opt" data-correct="0">"María is a worker" — it needs <span class="es">ser</span> <span class="mk">❌</span></button>
        </div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-q1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 3 SUBJECT PRONOUNS -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Who is doing it?</span>
      <h2>The six persons</h2>
      <p>Every verb has six "persons". You'll rarely <b>write</b> the pronoun (the ending shows who), but you must know them to pick the right ending:</p>
      <table class="conj" aria-label="The six subject pronouns">
        <tr><td>yo</td><td>I</td></tr>
        <tr><td>tú</td><td>you <span style="color:var(--soft);font-weight:500">(one friend)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>él / ella / usted</td><td>he / she / you <span style="color:var(--soft);font-weight:500">(formal)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>nosotros / nosotras</td><td>we</td></tr>
        <tr><td>vosotros / vosotras</td><td>you all <span style="color:var(--soft);font-weight:500">(Spain, informal)</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>ellos / ellas / ustedes</td><td>they / you all <span style="color:var(--soft);font-weight:500">(formal)</span></td></tr>
      </table>
      <p class="note"><b>Key:</b> <span class="es">usted</span> and <span class="es">ustedes</span> mean "you" but take the <b>él/ellos</b> endings — a polite quirk. Spanish normally <b>drops</b> the pronoun: just <span class="es">hablo</span>, not <span class="es">yo hablo</span>, unless you want emphasis or to avoid confusion.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4 REGULAR -AR -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Regular verbs · Step 1</span>
      <h2>-AR verbs: the biggest family</h2>
      <p>Take the infinitive <span class="es">hablar</span> (to speak), chop off <span class="hl r">-ar</span>, and add the endings:</p>
      <table class="conj" aria-label="hablar in the present tense">
        <caption><span class="es">hablar</span> — to speak</caption>
        <tr><th>person</th><th>form</th></tr>
        <tr><td>yo</td><td>habl<span class="chg">o</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>tú</td><td>habl<span class="chg">as</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>él / ella / usted</td><td>habl<span class="chg">a</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>nosotros</td><td>habl<span class="chg">amos</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>vosotros</td><td>habl<span class="chg">áis</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>ellos / ustedes</td><td>habl<span class="chg">an</span></td></tr>
      </table>
      <p>Same endings for every regular <span class="es">-ar</span> verb: <span class="es">trabajar</span> (work), <span class="es">estudiar</span> (study), <span class="es">escuchar</span> (listen), <span class="es">visitar</span> (visit).</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5 Q textans -ar -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag">Type it</span>
      <h2>Your turn — an -AR verb</h2>
      <div class="activity">
        <div class="qline"><span class="qnum">1</span><span>Write the <b>nosotros</b> ("we") form of <span class="es">trabajar</span> (to work).</span></div>
        <div class="textans" data-t="t1" data-answer="trabajamos">
          <input type="text" inputmode="text" maxlength="30" autocomplete="off" autocapitalize="off" spellcheck="false" aria-label="Your answer">
          <button class="checkbtn">Check ✓</button>
        </div>
        <div class="hint">Hint: chop <span class="es">-ar</span>, add the "we" ending <span class="es">-amos</span>.</div>
        <div class="feedback" id="fb-t1"></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 6 REGULAR -ER / -IR -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Regular verbs · Step 2</span>
      <h2>-ER and -IR verbs</h2>
      <p>These two families share <b>all</b> their endings except <b>nosotros</b> and <b>vosotros</b>:</p>
      <div class="trio">
        <table class="conj" aria-label="comer in the present tense">
          <caption><span class="es">comer</span> — to eat</caption>
          <tr><td>como</td></tr><tr><td>comes</td></tr><tr><td>come</td></tr>
          <tr><td>com<span class="chg">emos</span></td></tr><tr><td>com<span class="chg">éis</span></td></tr><tr><td>comen</td></tr>
        </table>
        <table class="conj" aria-label="vivir in the present tense">
          <caption><span class="es">vivir</span> — to live</caption>
          <tr><td>vivo</td></tr><tr><td>vives</td></tr><tr><td>vive</td></tr>
          <tr><td>viv<span class="chg">imos</span></td></tr><tr><td>viv<span class="chg">ís</span></td></tr><tr><td>viven</td></tr>
        </table>
      </div>
