    <!-- 16 SER vs ESTAR -->
    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">The big split · ser vs estar</span>
      <h2>Two verbs for "to be"</h2>
      <p>Spanish has <b>two</b> verbs meaning "to be", and choosing wrongly changes your meaning. The rule of thumb:</p>
      <div class="bins" style="margin-bottom:14px">
        <div class="bin ren"><h3><span class="es" style="color:#9a6100">ser</span> — essence</h3>
          <p style="font-size:.98rem;margin:0">Permanent / defining facts: <b>identity, origin, nationality, job, what something is, physical &amp; character description, time &amp; date.</b></p></div>
        <div class="bin non"><h3><span class="es" style="color:#0a7a58">estar</span> — state</h3>
          <p style="font-size:.98rem;margin:0">Changeable / where: <b>location, moods &amp; feelings, health, temporary conditions,</b> and the present continuous.</p></div>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="hl s">Soy</span> profesora. — I <b>am</b> a teacher. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(job → ser)</span></li>
        <li>Madrid <span class="hl e">está</span> en España. — Madrid <b>is</b> in Spain. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(location → estar)</span></li>
        <li>Hoy <span class="hl e">estoy</span> cansado. — Today I <b>am</b> tired. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(feeling → estar)</span></li>
        <li><span class="hl s">Son</span> las tres. — It <b>is</b> three o'clock. <span style="color:var(--soft)">(time → ser)</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note warn"><b>Meaning changes:</b> <span class="es">ser aburrido</span> = to <b>be boring</b>; <span class="es">estar aburrido</span> = to <b>be bored</b>. <span class="es">ser listo</span> = to be <b>clever</b>; <span class="es">estar listo</span> = to be <b>ready</b>. Same adjective, different verb, different meaning.</p>
    </section>

