    <section class="screen">
      <span class="tag lo">Everyday present-tense phrases</span>
      <h2>tener, hay and hace</h2>
      <p>Three high-frequency patterns use the present tense where English does something different:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b><span class="es">tener</span> for feelings &amp; age.</b> Spanish "has" the feeling: <span class="es">tengo <b>hambre</b></span> (I'm hungry), <span class="es">tengo <b>sed</b></span> (thirsty), <span class="es">tengo <b>frío/calor</b></span> (cold/hot), <span class="es">tengo <b>miedo</b></span> (afraid), <span class="es">tengo <b>15 años</b></span> (I'm 15).</li>
        <li><b><span class="es">hay</span> = "there is / there are".</b> One word for both: <span class="es">hay un problema</span>, <span class="es">hay muchos turistas</span>. And <span class="es">hay que</span> + infinitive = "one must" (<span class="es">hay que estudiar</span>).</li>
        <li><b><span class="es">hace</span> for weather.</b> <span class="es">hace calor / frío / sol / viento</span>; <span class="es">hace buen / mal tiempo</span>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="note warn"><b>Watch out:</b> never translate "I am hungry" word-for-word as <span class="hl r"><s>soy/estoy hambre</s></span>. Feelings like hunger, thirst, fear, cold and age use <span class="es">tener</span>.</p>
    </section>

